>  If we think there's a meaningful number of cases where the input's size can be determined up front and where it'd be smaller than the specified chunk size, adding a fast-path that just handles a single chunk seems ok. (It'd be good to validate the perf impact, though, regardless.)

I have concerns about that, `source.Chunk(int.MaxValue)` will have different observable behaviour depending on whether `source` is countable or not (successfully returning a single chunk vs throwing OOM). That sounds like a violation of LSP to me. 

We might want to consider solving the issue in the general case using `List<T>` to build the chunks. It would result in a few additional allocations/copying but like you said the ship has sailed when it comes to peformance. It might look like the following (NB this hasn't been tested):
```C#
private static IEnumerable<TSource[]> ChunkIterator<TSource>(IEnumerable<TSource> source, int size)
{
    using IEnumerator<TSource> e = source.GetEnumerator();

    if (e.MoveNext())
    {
        var chunkBuilder = new List<TSource>(Math.Min(size, 1024)); // some cutoff constant after which resizes are acceptable.

        do
        {
            chunkBuilder.Add(e.Current);

            int i = 1;
            for (; i < size && e.MoveNext(); i++)
            {
                chunkBuilder.Add(e.Current);
            }

            yield return chunkBuilder.ToArray();

            if (i == size)
            {
                chunkBuilder.Clear();
            }
            else
            {
                yield break;
            }
        }
        while (e.MoveNext());
    }
}
```
